90
00:05:14,364 --> 00:05:20,130
There are a couple different task
states that a process can be in.

91
00:05:20,130 --> 00:05:25,870
So task running means that it's able to
run but it may not actually be executing.

92
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:27,287
And then task interruptible and

93
00:05:27,287 --> 00:05:29,760
uninterruptible are the two
different types of sleep.

94
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:33,546
Which as you could expect based
on our discussion of locking and

95
00:05:33,546 --> 00:05:36,056
wait queues are the two different types.

96
00:05:36,056 --> 00:05:39,460
That you specify with the kind
of function signature you use.

97
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:42,926
And whether or not you specify
interruptible in the function signature.

98
00:05:42,926 --> 00:05:45,470
And again,
those are going to determine whether or

99
00:05:45,470 --> 00:05:47,464
not you're going to wake up on signals.

100
00:05:47,464 --> 00:05:54,340
Or whether or not your code is going to
be reactivated on signals or ignore them.

101
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:58,488
And then the schedule function is the
function that actually runs the scheduler.

102
00:05:58,488 --> 00:06:01,221
And it's going to allow other
process to be scheduled,

103
00:06:01,221 --> 00:06:05,140
which will effectively put
the process that calls it to sleep.

104
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:09,507
So I mentioned before that
the wait_event_interruptible case and

105
00:06:09,507 --> 00:06:12,080
the reason why this flag not equal zero.

106
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:16,330
The reason why this is more than just
one check that's passed into a function.

107
00:06:16,330 --> 00:06:20,688
The fact that there's this macro magic
that's happening that's making this

108
00:06:20,688 --> 00:06:22,745
parameter getting re-evaluated.

109
00:06:22,745 --> 00:06:26,040
So a little bit more detail
about how this works.

